Moore's law stated that the number of transistors on a chip double every generation. This was falsely perceived that processor frequency doubling.
Amdahl's law: most of a program must be optimized or else the unoptimized portions will be a severe bottleneck, preventing the realization of much gain.

Floating point addition and multiplication are commutative but not associative.

Fortran first programming language, by John Backus.



# Compilers
x-86 has two assembly formats: AT&T and Intel. AT&T is the assumption.

x-86 is little-endian

`ebp` points to a frame record, which is formatted very similarly to that of AArch64: pointer caller's frame record and return address in that order from lowest memory address. Traverse through the stack record using `ebp`.

`l` suffix on the opcode (e.g., `addl`) means an instruction on longs

Function labels are typically prefixed with an `_`. 

<!-- TODO: is it some separate stack?? -->
When `call` is executed, the instruction after it (i.e., return address) will be pushed on (some) stack. `ret` will pop and write this value onto `PC`. 

The compiler only needs to implement a front-end for each ISA, and only one back-end in total.



# Measurements
Precision is consistency of the measurements. To counteract random error, do a bunch of trials and take the mean. Accuracy is the other dimension, and it can be undermined by systematic error.



# Algorithms
A clique is a dense graph, but every node is connected to every other node.

Diameter of graph: average distance between every pair of nodes.

Sparse graphs:
- Uniform-degree graphs: nodes have roughly same degree. High diameter graph.
- Power-law graphs: small number of very high degree nodes. Low diameter.

Six degrees of freedom. Experiment was mailing a letter, and having its recipients give it to people they know until it reached the target. Low diameter example.

Single-source shortest-path (SSSP) problem:
- Chaotic relaxation: random
- Dijkstra: poor parallelization
- Delta-stepping: allows some parallelization 
- Bellman-Ford: best paralleization, O(VE)



# Caches 
The arbitration unit only allows one core to use the bus at a time. Therefore, if multiple caches have the cache line, only one core can actually send the cache line.



# Parallelism
In high performance computing, the overhead of context switching is undesirable. Therefore, the rule is to keep the number of threads equal to the number of cores.

When the main thread calls `pthread_exit`, other threads continue to execute. 

Atomic operations expense depends on contention (dependencies and competition over resources like bus). No contention is as expensive as a write. Contention requires overhead to serialize the atomic operations.

The bus arbitration unit achieves mutual exclusion with atomic instructions. 

Barriers need to be reentrant, meaning the same threads can reach it an arbitrary amount of times.

The reason why the data-parallelism functions are not implemented in a library is because it's hard to think of a solution in terms of these primitives. Additionally, these generalized implementations suffer from materialization—the creation of many intermediate arrays. 

Store operations must wait in the store buffer, while loads can bypass because they don't have an equivalent buffer. 

Atomic instructions serve as a fence.



# Vectors
Vector processing started early in 1974; the ILLIAC IV is an array processor (spatial SIMD). The Cray-1 pioneered vector processors (temporal SIMD) the following year.

An alternate approach to lanes is a heavily pipelined.

Masked vector operations are also called predicated execution. 
 
Reduced Instruct Set Computer (RISC) (or load-store) does operations register to register and has separate instructions for load and store.

Stride k will not query the same bank if the number of banks is not divisible by k. Some designs have designated a number of banks that is prime, but this eliminates the possibility of using the address to get the line number, increasing the time it takes to calculate the bank to query.

Distances in loop dependence graphs indicate how many iterations before is the dependence reliant on (e.g., 0 means this iteration).

An arrow with a circle is an output dependence. Likewise, a perpendicular line is an anti dependence. Without marks is a flow dependence.



# MPI
Scaling out might be preferred over scaling up because adding more machines is cheaper than increasing the number of cores. Scaling out also have a higher aggregate memory ceiling.
